messages:
Noah R: Heads-up: multiple customers hit runtime errors when using our SDK in edge functions. Looks like undici got bundled into edge builds and fails at runtime. Want to settle on a default strategy.
Priya S: For DX I'd prefer we avoid shipping undici as a hard dependency that ends up in browser/edge bundles. Edge runtimes already have global fetch and add cost/size if we bundle undici.
Liam K: Agreed. But Node <18 really needs something. We should also consider Bun (has global fetch but different API quirks) and Deno.
Olga M: Also TypeScript consumers: lots of them don't enable DOM libs and then `fetch` type is missing. We need clear guidance rather than forcing consumer configs.
Priya S: Proposed rule: 1) Prefer globalThis.fetch when present and looks like a standard Fetch API. 2) Attempt dynamic fallback import of undici only in Node-like environments. 3) If fallback fails, throw a clear runtime error with exact remediation steps.
Liam K: Implementation notes: dynamic import keeps undici out of browser/edge bundles when bundlers respect dynamic import. But some packagers still include dynamic imports. We should publish undici as an optional peer/install instruction, not a hard dep.
Noah R: Could we use optionalDependencies? npm installs them by default; that still adds install size. Maybe better: document `npm i undici` and runtime attempt to import it.
Priya S: Example adapter (ESM-first, CJS-friendly):
```js
// fetch-adapter.mjs
export async function resolveFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;

  // Try require() first for older CJS setups
  try {
    // eslint-disable-next-line global-require
    const und = require('undici');
    if (und && typeof und.fetch === 'function') return und.fetch;
  } catch (e) {
    // ignore
  }

  // dynamic import fallback (works in ESM)
  try {
    const mod = await import('undici');
    if (mod && typeof mod.fetch === 'function') return mod.fetch;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(
      `@redwood/inference: no global fetch detected and dynamic import of 'undici' failed. Install undici (npm i undici) or provide a global fetch polyfill. See https://docs.redwood.ai/sdk/fetch for examples.`
    );
  }
}
```
Olga M: That `require` inside ESM file might blow up with bundlers but it's intended for native Node CJS consumers. We'll need a comment in docs about packaging edge vs server.
Liam K: For TypeScript typings we should export a small FetchLike type: `export type FetchLike = typeof fetch;` and in README show how consumers add `"lib": ["ES2020", "DOM"]` or add `/// <reference lib=\"dom\" />` at top of files. Offer an alternative: `import type { FetchLike } from '@redwood/inference';` if they can't enable DOM lib.
Priya S: Docs should include a small matrix: Node 16 (polyfill required), Node 18+ (global fetch available), Bun (global fetch but compat notes), Deno (global fetch), Vercel Edge / Cloudflare / Fastly (global fetch only).
Noah R: Error message wording suggestion (user-facing):
`Error: @redwood/inference requires a Fetch API implementation. On Node <18 run: npm install undici && node -r ./polyfill.js. On Edge runtimes use the runtime's global fetch. See: https://docs.redwood.ai/sdk/fetch`
Olga M: Add example polyfill file for Node entrypoint: `polyfill.js` -> `globalThis.fetch = (await import('undici')).fetch;` and show CJS variant: `(async () => { globalThis.fetch = (await import('undici')).fetch; })();` in docs.
Priya S: Packaging/publishing note: avoid putting undici in dependencies. Use peerDependencies? Peer is also noisy. I vote for no runtime dependency; make undici an explicit opt-in. That reduces surprising edge breakage.
Liam K: CI: add test matrix for bundle analysis to assert that undici isn't bundled into typical Next.js edge build. Also test Node 16 with explicit polyfill and Node 18 without.
DevEx Bot: CI update: added bundle-check job and runtime matrix (node16, node18, deno, vercel-edge). Current failures: nextjs-edge example accidentally pulled in undici via a transitive dev dep. Fix required.
Noah R: UX: in quickstart we should show three copy/paste snippets: Node 18 server (no-op), Node <18 (install undici + polyfill snippet), and Edge (do nothing, rely on runtime). Also include a Vite/Next bundler tip: use `exclude`/`noExternal` to avoid bundling server-only modules into edge function.
Olga M: Also propose adding an FAQ entry: "Why do I get: \"ReferenceError: fetch is not defined\"?" with exact repro steps and fixes. That cuts down on support tickets.
Priya S: Action items: Priya -> write SDK adapter + tests; Liam -> add bundle CI check + perf test; Olga -> draft docs + quickstarts + TS typing guidance; Noah -> coordinate PR and changelog entry.
Noah R: Thanks team. Let's keep the runtime detection code minimal and error messages prescriptive.
